23:02:53 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Great questions! Let’s break them down. ### Why is the cabin air so dry? At 35,000 ft, the outside air is extremely cold (around -55°C / -67°F) and has almost no water vapor—it’s incredibly dry, like desert air. The cabin is pressurized using air drawn from the engines (called bleed air). This air is compressed, heated, then cooled and delivered to the cabin. But no moisture is added; in fact, the process removes what little moisture there is to prevent corrosion, electrical problems, and condensation that could damage the aircraft structure. That’s why the relative humidity in the cabin usually sits between 5% and 20%—drier than most deserts. Your skin and eyes feel parched, and you may need extra water. ### If the cabin pressure “stays the same,” why do my ears pop? Your assumption that the cabin pressure stays exactly the same as on the ground isn’t quite right. The cabin is pressurized, but not to sea-level pressure. At cruising altitude, the cabin pressure is typically equivalent to the air pressure you’d feel at an altitude of about 1,800–2,400 meters (6,000–8,000 ft). That’s lower than ground pressure. During takeoff and climb, the outside pressure drops quickly, and the cabin pressure gradually decreases to that “8,000 ft cabin altitude.” Your ears sense this pressure drop, and the Eustachian tubes need to open to equalize—hence the pops, as air escapes the middle ear. During descent, the cabin pressure is gradually increased back toward ground level, so the outside pressure on your eardrum becomes higher than inside the middle ear, making your ears feel plugged until you yawn, swallow, or intentionally pop them to let air back in. So even though the cabin environment is controlled, it’s not constant—it follows a “pressure schedule” that changes enough to make your ears react, especially when you have a cold or sinus congestion.
